asures are working but we're not there  yet and so people should take heart that  the measures are working because it is  bringing this this epidemic under  control but also be resolute in that we  have we're not there yet and so we need  this this to continue and of course  we'll keep reviewing that advice and the  scientists the scientific advisory group  are meeting next week but for this  weekend the message is really crystal  clear which is to stay at home and in  terms of that messaging and you say it's  crystal clear I've got to say from a  personal perspective I've not found it  difficult to understand but there are  people who've said to me that the  government has not been specific enough  and particularly when we're looking at  last weekend and no doubt we will see  some of it this weekend sadly of people  not really following the rules do you  have to be even more precise and say if  you go out this weekend you go out for  an hour tops  you must keep moving you cannot sit down  you cannot have a barbecue you can't sit  down with a flask of tea whatever it is  do you have to be absolutely crystal  clear and all that advice well I think  we have been we I was asked last weekend  whether it was okay to to stop and have  a picnic and the answers that it is is  truly no because the reason that you can  go out for exercise is to get exercise  and because of the very positive  benefits that you get from exercise so  we have been crystal clear as as you say  the vast vast majority of people are  following the rules and Memorial II  pleased over the last week or so to see  that the number of people following the  rules which we track and the proportion  and there hasn't been as slackening off  people are continuing to follow the very  stringent rules despite the impact  it has and we understand that I  understand it and and so actually the  the amount of adherence to the social  distancing rules is something that I'm  very pleased about of course I always  want to see you know everybody following  and there is a very small minority who  flout it but actually if you look at the  figures and if you look at the data that  we use to try to measure these things  there is a very very high degree of  support for the measures and adherence  to the measure and so I you know I want  to thank people who do follow the  measures and then of course along with  the police we we try to get that  proportion increasing all the time there  is some very confusing messaging not  from the government but from all these  sort of academics and things in all this  when we've been talking about a vaccine  because we heard only a week or so ago  people with some experts are saying a  vaccine was going to be middle of next  year in The Times today there's this  article coming from from a very  high-ranking scientists in Oxford's  talking about the possibility absolutely  pushing us to the limits but we might be  able to get something into production by  September clearly you're across all the  research door the work that's going on  here what what do you understand a  realistic timetable to be like I am I am  engaged in in all of that and I know of  the I know quite a lot about the Oxford  project and it is really great to see  some hope on the especially on the front  page of the newspapers and but the way  that I think about it is as follows and  we will put all the resources we  possibly can into getting a vaccine  because of the massive benefits that  we'd have to have our vaccine and we're  also putting enormous resources into  making sure that when a vaccine comes  about if and when then we can  manufacture enough here to be able to  get it to everybody as quickly as  possible because it's not just about  discovering the vaccine and then  checking that it's safe  but it's also then about manufacturing  it and we have got vaccine manufacturing  capability in this country but it needs  expanding so we're doing that in  parallel once we wait for the scientists  to develop a vaccine that we're happy  with and then essentially to make sure  that whatever the outcome of the science  however fast that happens and we are  ready ready to get the regulatory  approvals ready to get the production  and ready to get it logistically to  everybody who needs it so in a sense my  job is not to have a particularly to  have a view on how fast that will happen  and I think the truth is it's impossible  to know but it's but it's nice to see  some some hope as I say my job is to  make sure that as and when the  scientists make their breakthroughs and  you know there's no way I could do the  science I leave that to them and the  country is ready to step up pick up the  science turn it into mass production and  get it out and and that there aren't any  barriers in the way whether financial or  regulatory or in terms of capabilities  to the scientists making the fastest  progress that they possibly can but  there are some barriers in place I mean  there's been talk a lot of talk about  this plasma therapy in order to combat  covert 19 the government had not  approved trials for plasma therapy why  is that well if an individual clinician  recommends it then that can be used and  we are expanding the trials of different  of different medicines different  therapies all of the time in fact we  have one of the biggest trials program  in the whole world going on in this  country for instance you might heard of  hydroxychloroquine which is much  discussed and we have one of the biggest  trials of hydroxychloroquine  in the world and and in the same way  that we follow the scientific advice on  social distancing so - we're determined  to follow the scientific advice and  follow the science on making sure we get  these treatments in get them in in a  within a clinical trials  we collect the data we really understand  what works and what doesn't work what  sort of dosage apply to which patients  at what time so that we can then learn  from them and treat more people more  effectively so you know the one that you  mentioned is a is something that I've  seen and I know is available to the  right people on the NHS where that's  where that's clinically appropriate  those decisions for any individual  patient of course have got to be made by  the clinician who's treating look I want  to talk to you about PPE in just a  second before we get to that though can  we can we talk about the number of  people on the front line who have caught  and sadly passed away from kovat 19 what  sort of figures are we looking at at the  moment well at the moment 19 people have  died from the NHS family the yeah the  true for those for them it is a and it's  a heartbreaking heartbreaking story and  I know that it affects everybody who  works in the NHS to see their their  colleagues died and I think that the the  important thing is to work out to what  extent people contracted it in the line  of duty so to speak because of course  you know people can have caught it from  all sorts of different reasons and you  mentioned pee pee wee wee wee aren't  aware of any link from shortages of PPE  to any of these these deaths but my  heart goes out to their families and and  I I've said already that I think the  fact that such a high proportion also  are from people who move to this country  to work for the NHS is a it's really  heart rending to see people who've given  they're literally given their lives for  the NHS who moved here and to give that  service and and and I think we should  pay tribute to them  what absolutely I think everyone would  agree with you on that but in terms of  of the PPE there was now I heard you in  the news conference last night I have to  say it did strike me a little bit and it  no doubt it's a valid point to say look  the PPE s here there's a logistic issue  you're getting it around the the 58,000  bases whatever you mentioned they but  there was the has been certainly  reported on Twitter and elsewhere 